```javascript
// Before: connection was never released if run() threw
async function processOrder(pool, order, run) {
  const conn = pool.acquire();
  const result = await run(conn, order);
  pool.release(conn);
  return result;
}

// After: connection is always released in finally block
async function processOrder(pool, order, run) {
  const conn = pool.acquire();
  try {
    const result = await run(conn, order);
    return result;
  } finally {
    pool.release(conn);
  }
}
```

The leak happened because `processOrder` acquired a connection but didn't wrap it in try/finally. When order validation failed (throwing an error), the release never ran. Each failed order consumed a connection forever. Within hours, all 5 connections in the pool were stuck in-use, and the service crashed with timeouts.

All four tests pass now — orders that fail validation still release their connections, concurrent failures don't leak, and healthy orders work fine.
